and vectorizable across j. That might improve performance significantly.\n\nSimilarly, the sliding update already does that: one row at a time with SIMD over columns.\n\nSo we can change initial column sum computation to loop over rows first, then columns with SIMD. Also maybe combine initialization of v to zero? We need v[j] = 0 before adding s rows. Could do:\n#pragma omp simd\nfor j: v[j]=0;\nthen for k in 0..s-1:\n    const int* rowPtr = &a[(base+k)*n];\n    #pragma omp simd\n    for j: v[j] += rowPtr[j];\n\nThis is more cache-friendly and vectorizable.\n\nAlso, the prefix computation could be done with a simple loop; maybe we can use OpenMP SIMD? Not possible due to recurrence. But we could try to compute horizontal max without prefix using sliding window that has dependency but can be unrolled manually? Probably not better.\n\nAnother idea: Use two-level tiling for cache? Maybe not needed.\n\nGiven current performance ~0.012s, baseline 0.133s, already good. We want to improve further maybe by a few percent.\n\nLet's propose the transformation of initial column sum loop restructuring. Also perhaps add `#pragma omp simd` on prefix? No.\n\nAlso we can try to use `restrict` qualifiers more aggressively: vp is already restrict in update, but not in initial loop. We could declare pointers with __restrict for a and v.\n\nLet's write new solution.cpp accordingly.\n\nWe must ensure correctness: same algorithm, just different loop order. Should be correct.\n\nAlso maybe we can avoid recomputing pref from scratch each row by maintaining horizontal window sums incrementally? But that would introduce dependency; current approach is fine.\n\nAnother possible improvement: Use `#pragma omp simd` on the prefix loop? Not legal due to recurrence, but compiler might still vectorize with reductions? No.\n\nLet's implement:\n\n- In initial column sum:\n```\n#pragma omp simd\nfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) v[j] = 0;\nfor (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n    const int* __restrict rowPtr = &a[